#: ../../source/braces_README.md:295
msgid "What is \"brace expansion\"?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/braces_README.md:297
msgid ""
"Brace expansion is a type of parameter expansion that was made popular by"
" unix shells for generating lists of strings, as well as regex-like "
"matching when used alongside wildcards (globs)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/braces_README.md:299
msgid ""
"In addition to \"expansion\", braces are also used for matching. In other"
" words:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/braces_README.md:301
msgid "[brace expansion](#brace-expansion) is for generating new lists"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/braces_README.md:302
msgid "[brace matching](#brace-matching) is for filtering existing lists"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/braces_README.md:307
msgid "There are two main types of brace expansion:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/braces_README.md:309
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"**lists**: which are defined using comma-separated values inside curly "
"braces: `{a,b,c}`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/braces_README.md:310
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"**sequences**: which are defined using a starting value and an ending "
"value, separated by two dots: `a{1..3}b`. Optionally, a third argument "
"may be passed to define a \"step\" or increment to use: `a{1..100..10}b`."
" These are also sometimes referred to as \"ranges\"."
msgstr ""

